  "premise": "Estate lawyer Bram Vane is summoned to read the will of Marisol Petra, a cartographer who vanished seven years ago and was declared dead. The will shouldn't exist—Marisol's body was never found—and the diary enclosed in her estate continues writing entries for dates after her disappearance, addressed directly to Bram. As he follows the diary's cipher through the decaying Petra estate and the coastal town that forgot her, Bram discovers Marisol may still be alive, hiding, while someone close to the original investigation kills to keep her secrets buried. The killer is the retired detective who declared the case closed, and the climax comes when Bram deciphers the diary's final entry, lures the killer back to the estate's flooded map room, and finds Marisol herself waiting in the dark. The novel ends with Marisol legally resurrected, her estate dissolved into a found family of the people the town abandoned, and Bram choosing to stay rather than close the file.",
  "about_author": "A. M. Quintana is a former probate clerk who spent a decade reading other people's final wishes before writing them into fiction. They live in a coastal town much like the one in this book, where the fog rolls in early and everyone has one story they refuse to tell.",
